This file descibes the CPU and instruction set, and related features.

Memory can be mapped as tagged memory or untagged memory. Each byte of
tagged memory has two tag bits, and each general 64-bit register has one
tag bit. These are used for "capabilities", but do not confuse the CPU
capability values with the operating system capabilities, which are
different things but are related (operating system capabilities are
represented using capability values in the CPU and in memory). In a
register, the tag bit is set for a capability value or clear for a
number. In memory, a capability value must be exactly eight bytes long,
with the first tag bit set in the first seven bytes and the second tag
bit set in the last seven bytes. If either tag bit is set but does not
follow this pattern, then it is a broken and unusable capability value.

You cannot do anything with capability values in application mode other
than check whether or not it is a capability value and testing them for
equality with other capability values, and copying them to/from memory
(with restrictions) and general-purpose 64-bit registers; you cannot
inspect the actual value of them, nor create them; therefore the values
are opaque to the application program. In system mode, it is possible to
avoid these restrictions.

A broken capability value in memory cannot be read into a register, but
it is possible to create a broken capability value in memory by overwriting
some of the bytes but not others, therefore making it broken. When copying
memory, it is an error if the second tag bit of the first byte of the
source is set or if the first tag bit of the last byte of the source is
set (this restriction is so that you cannot create a capability value by
combining pieces of two different capabilities).

All execution in application mode is deterministic; there aren't any
instructions that can produce non-deterministic results (which are visible
to the application program) in application mode. It must call the operating
system to do any such things as I/O, including timing, random numbers, etc.

